From: challoc <noreply@chainguard.dev>
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] memefficiency: start the replicaof flush defrag test lower
 under challoc

"Active defrag can't be triggered during replicaof database flush. See
issue #14267" needs active defrag to start on what the sync's flush
leaves: the flush runs within a few ms of REPLICAOF, before the next
cron tick. The bundled jemalloc leaves about 22 MB of fragmentation
there. challoc returns the freed slots (an epoch write runs the main
thread's cache GC, challoc PR #576, merged), leaving 1.6 to 3.4 MB, so at the
stock 2mb active-defrag-ignore-bytes defrag sometimes never starts ("defrag
not started."). Under MALLOC=challoc the replica starts at 1mb and goes
back to 2mb once defrag runs.

The change goes through a helper, challoc_or, which returns the challoc
value when mem_allocator contains "challoc" and the stock value
otherwise, so the bundled-jemalloc build runs the file unchanged.

Not calibrated: the tests whose targets challoc misses because of its
slab floor ("eval scripts", "IDMP streams", the three "HFE" tests, "argv
retained by the main thread from IO thread", "big list", "template-encoded
hashes"). challoc keeps at least one 64 KiB slab per size class in use,
and a few long-lived small objects can hold many such slabs, so after
defrag it leaves 1.4 to 5 MB that no pass can recover, where jemalloc
leaves 0.5 to 2.5 MB. That is a challoc issue, not the tests'. Neither is
"main dictionary", which needs challoc PR #576, or "big keys", whose
defrag-cycle latency is intermittently 14 to 33 ms against its 30 ms
limit under challoc. The slab floor is tracked by wolfi-dev/challoc#604.
---
 tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl | 23 ++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl b/tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl
--- a/tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl
+++ b/tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl
@@ -85,6 +85,17 @@ test "Active defrag handles equal fragmentation thresholds" {
 } {} {defrag external:skip tsan:skip standalone}
 
 run_solo {defrag} {
+    # challoc_or CHALLOC_VALUE VALUE: CHALLOC_VALUE under MALLOC=challoc
+    # (mem_allocator contains "challoc"), VALUE otherwise, for a figure a
+    # test takes from the bundled jemalloc's behaviour where challoc's
+    # differs. The bundled-jemalloc build runs the file unchanged.
+    proc challoc_or {challoc_value value} {
+        if {[string match {*challoc*} [s mem_allocator]]} {
+            return $challoc_value
+        }
+        return $value
+    }
+
     proc wait_for_defrag_stop {maxtries delay {expect_frag 0}} {
         wait_for_condition $maxtries $delay {
             [s active_defrag_running] eq 0 && ($expect_frag == 0 || [s allocator_frag_ratio] <= $expect_frag)
@@ -1429,7 +1440,16 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
                 $replica config set active-defrag-threshold-lower 5
                 $replica config set active-defrag-cycle-min 65
                 $replica config set active-defrag-cycle-max 75
-                $replica config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes 2mb
+                # challoc: the sync's flush runs within a few ms of
+                # REPLICAOF, before the next cron tick, so defrag starts on
+                # what the flush leaves. The bundled jemalloc leaves about
+                # 22 MB of fragmentation there; challoc returns the freed
+                # slots (an epoch write runs the main thread's cache GC),
+                # leaving 1.6 to 3.4 MB, and at 2mb defrag sometimes never
+                # starts. Start at 1mb, and go back to 2mb once defrag
+                # runs: at 1mb it would keep running on challoc's slab
+                # floor, a known challoc issue this file does not calibrate.
+                $replica config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes [challoc_or 1mb 2mb]
 
                 # add a mass of string keys
                 set count 0
@@ -1468,6 +1488,7 @@ run_solo {defrag} {
                         puts [$replica memory malloc-stats]
                         fail "defrag not started."
                     }
+                    $replica config set active-defrag-ignore-bytes 2mb
 
                     wait_for_sync $replica
 
